{"rulings":[{"amendment_id":"fr-4-4f43dda9","decision":"reject","rationale":"The failing test is real, but the amendment’s diagnosis and proposed fix target the wrong code path. In `SaveFile::from_value` the loaded state is mutated before engine construction by `normalize_loaded_carbonate_state()` and only then passed through the habitat refresh path. Immediate post-load comparison shows the first drift in carbonate cache fields (`water.ph` and `water.bicarbonate_mg_total`), while the habitat-refresh inputs and derived habitat terms stay aligned; that makes the carbonate normalization path the supported cause, not `Engine::from_parts`. The proposed fix to skip or special-case `state.refresh_habitat_registry()` is also at odds with the explicit current-schema test that load must rebuild stale habitat registries (`current_schema_load_rebuilds_stale_habitat_registry`). Because the amendment prescribes the wrong fix surface and risks breaking intentional load repair behavior, it should be rejected."}],"summary":"Reject the disputed amendment. The save/load determinism regression exists, but the evidence points to load-time carbonate normalization rather than habitat-registry refresh, so the amendment’s proposed remedy is not supported and would undermine an intentional stale-registry repair path."}